      "claim": "In a rabbit meningitis model caused by S. pneumoniae, oritavancin demonstrated low CNS penetration, with CSF penetration only 5% of plasma levels, indicating inadequate CNS access for glycopeptide antibiotics.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C09"],
      "claim": "In rabbits, [14C]dalbavancin-derived radioactivity in CSF was very low and measurable only at 12 and 24 h postdose, indicating poor BBB permeability for this glycopeptide.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "high",
  "decision_summary": "The query is a lipoglycopeptide closely related to dalbavancin and oritavancin. Direct evidence in meningitis models shows these agents have poor CSF penetration (1-5% or trace), indicating inadequate CNS access. Small-molecule glycoside brain-penetration data are not transferable due to major structural differences.",
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct experimental data for the query molecule; extrapolation from close structural analogues.",
    "Meningitis may increase BBB permeability, but the magnitude remains insufficient for glycopeptides."
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [],
  "revision_action": "initial"
}

